{
 "manufacturer": "Circutor",
 "model": "CVM-E3-MINI",
 "short_model": "CVM-E3-MINI",
 "aliases": [
  "CVM-E3-MINI-ITF-RS485",
  "CVM-E3-MINI-MC",
  "CVM-E3-MINI-FLEX",
  "CVM-E3-MINI-mV",
  "CVM-E3-MINI-ITF-WiEth"
 ],
 "equipment_type": "Power meter",
 "applies_to": "Circutor CVM-E3-MINI DIN-rail power analyzer, RS-485 Modbus RTU (Map 2 / CVM-C10-compatible addresses), manual M170B01-03-25B.",
 "summary": "The CVM-E3-MINI is a compact DIN-rail three-phase power analyzer from Circutor with an RS-485 port for Modbus RTU or BACnet. It exposes two parallel Modbus maps; this file uses Map 2, which follows the CVM-C10 layout shifted by 0x1000. Values are 32-bit integers with fixed scaling (V x10, mA, Hz x100) read with function 03/04. Included: voltages, currents, reactive and apparent power, THD, demand, total energies, status words, setup registers and reset commands.",
 "transport": [
  "RTU"
 ],
 "defaults": {
  "unit_id": 1,
  "baud": 19200,
  "parity": "None",
  "data_bits": 8,
  "stop_bits": 1,
  "tcp_port": null
 },
 "addressing": {
  "convention": "hex 0-based",
  "note": "All addresses in the manual are hexadecimal wire addresses. Its function 04 example reads L1 voltage at 0000 (Map 1) and gets 0000 084D = 212.5 V. Map 2 adds 0x1000 to the CVM-C10 addresses, so L1 voltage is 0x1000 here."
 },
 "default_word_order": "ABCD",
 "firmware": "Notes in the source distinguish software version v2.xx (higher baud rates, address limit 247).",
 "source": {
  "title": "CVM-E3-MINI Power analyzer Instruction Manual",
  "doc_number": "M170B01-03-25B",
  "revision_date": null,
  "url": "https://docs.circutor.com/docs/M170B01-03.pdf",
  "pages": "Section 7.3, PDF pp. 87-103"
 },
 "notes": [
  "Word order: the manual's read example returns register 1 = 0000, register 2 = 084D for 212.5 V, so 32-bit values are high word first (ABCD).",
  "Map 1 (CVM-MINI compatible addresses) is not listed; the manual recommends Map 2 for new integrations. Map 1 also contains printing errors (e.g. min average current printed 106-417, frequency scale x10 vs x100).",
  "Left out: active power, reactive power (signed), power factor and cos phi, and kW maximum demand. The document does not say whether the Modbus values are signed, so the data type cannot be fixed.",
  "Left out: per-tariff and per-phase energies, harmonics, CO2/cost, alarm and pulse programming, password and BACnet registers; all are in tables 62-79 of the manual.",
  "Left out: the 'Generated apparent energy (VAh)' total, whose address is misprinted (10FE-10EF).",
  "Configuration changes made over Modbus apply only after a device reset; the ratio registers must be written as one group."
 ],
